I don't know if there is any standard vocabulary to make the
distinction.  When talking about C++, I normally use
"inheritance" for the OO design philosophy, and "derivation" for
the C++ technique which is used (most of the time) to implement
it.  Stressing that not all C++ derivation is OO inheritance
(and that C++ templates also permit duck-type inheritance, a la
Smalltalk, if the actual type can be resolved at compile time).

Whatever you call it, I think it is important to maintain a
distinction: regardless of the language used, I'll use some
"inheritance".  My first uses of inheritance, in fact, were in
assembler, before I even learned C (and before C++ even
existed---or I knew that the technique was called inheritance).

